# ADR 0002: Both halves ship from one repository

- Status: accepted
- Date: {{ date }}

## Context

A Tauri plugin is two artefacts: a crate the application registers on its
builder, and an npm package the webview imports. They agree on three spellings
- the plugin name, the command key, and the permission generated from it - and
a disagreement in any of them is not a build failure. It is a permission error
in the webview, at run time, in the application that depends on the plugin.

Splitting them into two repositories is possible and is what a Rust crate and a
TypeScript package would normally do.

## Decision

Both halves live here and are released under one tag. The crate publishes to
crates.io and the package to npm from the same workflow, at the same version.

## Consequences

A change that moves one half cannot be released without the other, and the two
tests that hold the shared strings - the Rust one asserting the registered name
and the TypeScript one asserting the `plugin:name|command` prefix - run in the
same gate.

The price is a repository with two toolchains in it, and a version number that
moves on npm because the Rust half changed. That is cheaper than a version pair
that has to be reasoned about by whoever installs the plugin.
